'''HD 189733 b''' is an [[exoplanet]] in the constellation of [[Vulpecula]] approximately {{Convert|64.5|ly|pc|lk=on|abbr=off}} away<ref name="GaiaDR3"/> from the [[Solar System]]. Astronomers in France discovered the planet orbiting the star [[HD 189733]] on October 5, 2005, by observing its [[Astronomical transit|transit]] across the star's face.<ref name="Bouchy2005"/> With a mass 11.2% higher than that of [[Jupiter]] and a radius 11.4% greater, HD 189733 b orbits its host star once every 2.2 days at an [[orbital speed]] of {{convert|152.0|km/s|mph|sigfig=4|abbr=off|sp=us}}, making it a [[hot Jupiter]] with poor prospects for [[extraterrestrial life]].<ref name="Bonomo2017"/>

The closest transiting hot Jupiter to Earth, HD 189733 b has been the subject of close atmospheric observation. Scientists have studied it with high- and low-resolution instruments, both from the ground and from space.<ref name="Khd"/> Researchers have found that the planet's weather includes raining molten glass. HD 189733 b was also the first exoplanet to have its [[heat map|thermal map]] constructed,<ref name="Knutson2007"/><ref name="Majeau2012"/> possibly to be detected through [[polarimetry]],<ref name="Berdyugina2011"/> its overall color determined (deep blue),<ref name="Berdyugina2011"/><ref name="Evans2013"/> its transit viewed in the X-ray spectrum, and to have [[carbon dioxide]] confirmed as being present in its atmosphere.

In July 2014, [[NASA]] announced the discovery of very dry [[Exoplanet#Atmosphere|atmospheres]] on three exoplanets that orbited Sun-like stars: HD 189733 b, [[HD 209458 b]], and [[WASP-12b]].<ref name="NASA-20140724"/>

== Detection and discovery ==
